; Run the script of every NPC for one turn (the script interpreter)
;
; Called by #R$96B3 after each player command. Goes through the NPC states
; (NpcStates, 17 records of 7 bytes, $FF ends) and lets each NPC with an id do
; commands from its script until one of them succeeds or it has failed six times.
; .
; Per NPC. Subject is the NPC's id (a record with id 0 is a dormant slot that
; events fill in, and is skipped), its record goes to ActorRecord, its place to
; ActorLocation. MessagesVisible is cleared and set to 1 only when the player
; can see the NPC act: the NPC is within the player's reach (#R$9E25) and the
; player is not in the dark. In the dark, the first NPC within reach makes
; SightState go from 1 to 2 and "You hear a noise." is printed once for the turn
; (after that nothing of the NPCs is printed). NpcFailures counts the failures of
; the NPC. An NPC that is held by something (its record's holder is not
; AT_LOCATION) is passed to #R$9B16: the NPC acts as usual when the holder is
; dead or disabled or is a character; otherwise it does nothing, unless the
; holder is open or alive, when it tries to climb out (command $37) instead.
; ActorHasOrder is 1 when the NPC owns a stored order (given with SAY TO,
; #R$7F10). At the end the Subject is the player again, MessagesVisible is 1
; and ActorRecord points to the player's record.
; .
; THE SCRIPT LANGUAGE. An NPC state record is: +0 id, +1 the highest entry number
; for random choices, +2 word, the current script (address of the next command),
; +4 word, the behaviour table, +6 not used by the interpreter. A script is a
; row of commands in $C82D-$CA83. The first byte of a command is the command
; byte: bits 0-3 are the op, bit 4 says that a word follows the command, bit 5
; (SCRIPT_BIT_ERASE) erases the command byte after a success (#R$9928), bit 6
; (SCRIPT_BIT_NO_ORDER) says that a stored order must not replace the command,
; bit 7 is not used. The word of bit 4 is the ALTERNATIVE address, a script
; address: where the NPC goes when the command fails (it is the last two bytes
; of the command, and the command is 2 bytes longer). The ops:
; .
; 0 and 2: verb, object 1, object 2 (4 bytes). The NPC does the command as if it were typed
; (a verb id from the command table, $FF as an object means none; ids of
; objects and characters are in the ids list). #R$9928, #R$99C6.
; .
; 1 and 3: routine word, one unused byte (4 bytes). The routine is called in two passes like a
; verb handler and must set ActionAllowed to 1 in the first to agree. #R$9928.
; .
; 4: verb (2 bytes). The command without objects: a move (verbs 1-10 are
; the directions), or $24 (run) and $30 (capture, default handler #R$A3E6). A verb of $FF does nothing: the
; NPC is done for the turn, or jumps to the alternative address when there is
; one. #R$9974.
; .
; 5-9, B and D: end of the script (1 byte; $07 is the one used). The NPC takes
; the script of entry 0 of its behaviour table (the default script, #R$9A68) and
; is done for this turn.
; .
; C: behaviour id (2 bytes). Takes the script of the entry with that id in the
; behaviour table (#R$9AA0) and goes on with it at once.
; .
; E: script address (3 bytes). Jumps there and goes on with it at once.
; .
; F: highest entry number (2 bytes). Takes the script of a random entry of the behaviour table, from 0 to
; the smaller of this number and the record's +1 (#R$9A59), and goes on at once.
; .
; The behaviour table of an NPC is a row of (id, script word) entries ended by
; $FF. Entries 0 to the record's +1 have id 0 and are the scripts that op F and
; the end ops choose among (entry 0 is the default). The entries after them
; have as their id a command id: when a living NPC is the first or second object of
; a command that was just done, the entry for that verb (15 attack, 29 give, 48
; capture) becomes its script (#R$95DF, #R$9AA0).
; .
; A turn. The NPC takes the command at its current script address. Ops C, E and F cost
; no time. A command of op 0-4 is tried: when it succeeds the script address is
; already past it and the NPC is done for the turn. When it fails the failure
; counter NpcFailures goes up; the NPC then goes to the alternative address of the
; command if it has one, or to the next command; at 6 failures the NPC is done for
; the turn and goes on from there next turn. A command is a failure when the test pass
; of the command refuses it (#R$99C6).
; .
; Stored orders. If the NPC owns a stored order (ActorHasOrder = 1) and the command
; to be tried is an op 0-4 without bit 6, the order is taken first (#R$7F1A, once
; per turn). If it parses and is allowed, the NPC does that order instead (#R$99CE)
; and is done for the turn: the script address does not move. Otherwise the script command is tried.
; .
; Hardware: none itself; the commands print through the message printer.
@label=RunNpcScripts
c$980E CALL $9B02       ; SightState, PlayerLocation
 $9811 LD IY,$CACB      ; the first NPC state
*$9815 XOR A
 $9816 LD ($980B),A     ; no failures yet (NpcFailures)
 $9819 LD A,(IY+$00)    ; NPC id
@isub=CP END_MARK
 $981C CP $FF           ; the end of the list?
 $981E JP Z,$9909       ; yes: finish
 $9821 CP $00           ; 0: a dormant slot
 $9823 JP Z,$9901       ; skip it
 $9826 LD ($B6EA),A     ; Subject = the NPC
 $9829 CALL $9F82       ; A = its location, IX = its record
 $982C LD ($B70C),IX    ; ActorRecord
 $9830 LD ($B6F6),A     ; ActorLocation (also $FF if it has several)
 $9833 SUB A            ; MessagesVisible = 0
 $9834 LD ($B702),A
 $9837 LD A,(IY+$00)    ; the NPC id again
 $983A PUSH IY          ; keep the NPC state
 $983C LD IY,$C11B      ; IY = the player's record
 $9840 CALL $9E25       ; can the player reach the NPC?
 $9843 POP IY
 $9845 JR Z,$9868       ; no: nothing is seen
 $9847 LD A,($980C)     ; SightState
@isub=CP SIGHT_NOISE_HEARD
 $984A CP $02           ; the noise was heard: nothing is seen
 $984C JR Z,$9868
 $984E LD A,$01         ; the player sees what the NPC does
 $9850 LD ($B702),A     ; MessagesVisible = 1
 $9853 LD A,($980C)     ; SightState
@isub=CP SIGHT_DARK
 $9856 CP $01           ; lit: that is all
 $9858 JR NZ,$9868
 $985A INC A            ; dark: SightState = 2 (SIGHT_NOISE_HEARD)
 $985B LD ($980C),A
 $985E LD HL,$B027      ; "You hear a noise."
 $9861 CALL $72DD
 $9864 SUB A            ; MessagesVisible = 0 again
 $9865 LD ($B702),A
*$9868 LD A,$FF
 $986A CP (IX+$01)      ; is the NPC held by something?
 $986D JP NZ,$9B16      ; yes: #R$9B16 decides
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$9B16.
@label=RunNpcScripts_Start
*$9870 LD IX,($B70C)    ; IX = ActorRecord
 $9874 CALL $7F10       ; does the NPC own a stored order? (Z = yes)
 $9877 LD A,$00
 $9879 JR NZ,$987C
 $987B INC A            ; ActorHasOrder = 1
*$987C LD ($B6F4),A     ; ActorHasOrder
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$9974.
@label=RunNpcScripts_Script
*$987F LD L,(IY+$02)    ; HL = the current script
 $9882 LD H,(IY+$03)
; This entry point is used by the routine at #R$9974.
@label=RunNpcScripts_Command
*$9885 LD A,($980B)     ; NpcFailures: failures so far
@isub=CP SCRIPT_MAX_FAILURES
 $9888 CP $06           ; six: the NPC is done for this turn
 $988A JR Z,$9901
 $988C LD A,(HL)        ; the command byte
 $988D LD DE,$0004      ; DE = length of op 0-3 commands
 $9890 PUSH HL
 $9891 POP IX           ; IX = the command
@isub=AND SCRIPT_OP_MASK
 $9893 AND $0F          ; the op
@isub=CP SCRIPT_OPS_COMMANDS
 $9895 CP $05           ; 5 and over: control ops
 $9897 JR NC,$98CB
 $9899 LD A,($B6F4)     ; ActorHasOrder
 $989C CP $01           ; no stored order: the script command
 $989E JR NZ,$98BF
@isub=BIT SCRIPT_BIT_NO_ORDER,(HL)
 $98A0 BIT 6,(HL)       ; this command is not to be interrupted
 $98A2 JR NZ,$98BF
 $98A4 SUB A            ; the order is looked at once per turn
 $98A5 LD ($B6F4),A
 $98A8 INC A            ; A = 1: parse and test the order
 $98A9 CALL $7F1A       ; RunStoredOrder
 $98AC JR Z,$98BF       ; no usable order: the script command
 $98AE LD A,$01
 $98B0 LD ($B6FA),A     ; ActionPerform = 1
 $98B3 LD ($B6FB),A     ; ActionAllowed = 1
 $98B6 LD HL,$9901      ; after the order: the next NPC
 $98B9 PUSH HL          ; (return address of the jump)
 $98BA PUSH IX          ; (IX is popped by #R$99C6)
 $98BC JP $99CE         ; do the order at once, the test is done
*$98BF LD A,(HL)
 $98C0 AND $0F
@isub=CP SCRIPT_OP_VERB
 $98C2 CP $04           ; op 4: a verb alone
 $98C4 JP Z,$9974
 $98C7 JR C,$9928       ; op 0-3: #R$9928
 $98C9 JR $9901
@isub=CP SCRIPT_OP_JUMP
*$98CB CP $0E           ; E: jump
 $98CD JR NZ,$98DD
 $98CF LD E,(IX+$01)    ; the script address of the command
 $98D2 LD (IY+$02),E    ; is the current script
 $98D5 LD E,(IX+$02)
 $98D8 LD (IY+$03),E
 $98DB JR $987F         ; go on there
@isub=CP SCRIPT_OP_SWITCH
*$98DD CP $0C           ; C: switch behaviour
 $98DF JR NZ,$98EC
 $98E1 LD B,(IX+$01)    ; B = the behaviour id
 $98E4 LD A,(IY+$00)    ; A = the NPC id
 $98E7 CALL $9AA0       ; SwitchNpcScript
 $98EA JR $987F         ; go on with the new script
@isub=CP SCRIPT_OP_RANDOM
*$98EC CP $0F           ; F: random script
 $98EE JR NZ,$98F5
 $98F0 CALL $9A59       ; ChooseRandomScript
 $98F3 JR $987F         ; go on with it
*$98F5 CP $00           ; never 0 here (the op is 5 or more): always jumps
 $98F7 JR NZ,$98FC
 $98F9 ADD HL,DE
 $98FA JR $9885
*$98FC SUB A            ; 5-9, B, D: end of the script
 $98FD LD E,A           ; entry 0 of the behaviour table
 $98FE CALL $9A68       ; SelectScript: the default script
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$9928, #R$9974 and #R$9B16.
@label=RunNpcScripts_Next
@isub=LD DE,NPC_RECORD_SIZE
*$9901 LD DE,$0007      ; the next NPC
 $9904 ADD IY,DE
 $9906 JP $9815
*$9909 SUB A            ; Subject = the player
 $990A LD ($B6EA),A
 $990D INC A
 $990E LD ($B702),A     ; MessagesVisible = 1
 $9911 LD HL,$C11B      ; ActorRecord = the player's record
 $9914 LD ($B70C),HL
 $9917 RET
